  [Incentives for small families],
  [Rewards — easier loans, welfare benefits — make the two-child family attractive.],
  [Must encourage, never punish the poor.],
  [Education, especially of women],
  [Educated women marry later, plan children better and use contraception more readily.],
  [Takes a generation to show its full effect.],
  [Raising the marriage age],
  [Fewer child-bearing years per couple; the law sets 18 for women, 21 for men.],
  [A law on paper needs enforcement to matter.],
  [Easy contraceptive access],
  [Free, nearby choices remove the excuse of distance and cost.],
  [Services must stay confidential and respectful.],
)

- *Stabilisation has momentum:* even after birth rates fall, the total
  population keeps rising for years, because so many couples are young and
  yet to start families.
- *The syllabus pairing:* this unit puts amniocentesis and population
  strategies in one line — one is a diagnostic test that was misused, the
  other a nation that must be steered.
- *The honest scale:* awareness changed faster than numbers — behaviour
  change is slow, which is exactly why education gets the long budget in
  every population policy.

= Contraception I — Natural and Barrier

A couple wants to delay their first child for two years. Another has two
children and wants none more. Both need the same thing: methods that prevent
pregnancy *without harming health*. That is the whole idea of *contraception*
— the deliberate prevention of pregnancy. The simplest families of methods
need no hormones and no surgery: one family reads the body's calendar, the
other puts a physical wall between sperm and egg.

- *The principle:* a good contraceptive method should be safe, affordable,
  easy to use and free of long-term side effects — no method is perfect, so
  couples pick what suits their health and plans.
- *Natural methods* rely on timing or simple behaviour — zero cost, but the
  highest failure rate of all families.
- *Barrier methods* physically stop the sperm from reaching the egg — cheap,
  reliable when used properly, and the only family that also blocks infections.

== The natural (behavioural) methods

- *Periodic abstinence:* the couple avoids intercourse during the fertile
  window of the menstrual cycle — roughly *days 10 to 17* of a 28-day cycle —
  because ovulation (the release of an egg from the ovary) happens around
  day 14.
- *Withdrawal (coitus interruptus):* the male withdraws before ejaculation,
  the release of semen, so sperm do not enter the female tract. Discipline
  decides success, and it often fails.
- *Lactational amenorrhoea:* intense, *exclusive* breastfeeding keeps the
  mother's ovulation switched off, so menstrual periods stop (*amenorrhoea*)
  and pregnancy is unlikely — but only for about *six months*, and only while
  feeding is frequent and no other milk or food is given.
- *The honest warning:* natural methods carry the *highest failure rate*
  among all contraceptive families, because they depend on perfect timing and
  perfect self-control.

#worked-example("Worked Example — Finding the Fertile Window")[
  A woman has a regular 28-day cycle. Her last period began on the 1st of the
  month. The couple wants to practise periodic abstinence.

  - *Step 1 — count the days:* cycle days are counted from the *first day of
    menstrual flow*, so the 1st is day 1.
  - *Step 2 — locate ovulation:* in a 28-day cycle ovulation falls near
    *day 14*, when a mature egg leaves the ovary.
  - *Step 3 — add margins:* sperm can survive in the female tract for about
    *3 to 5 days*, and the egg lives about *1 day*. So pregnancy is possible
    from about day 10 to day 17.
  - *Step 4 — the rule:* avoid intercourse from *day 10 to day 17* — the
    fertile window this method treats as unsafe.
]

== The barrier methods

#table(
  columns: (1fr, 1.5fr, 1.3fr),
  table.header[*Method*][*How it works*][*Extra benefit / limit*],
  [Male condom], [A thin rubber sheath rolled over the penis; it collects semen and blocks sperm entry.],
  [Also blocks STIs — cheap and easily available; must be used every time.],
  [Female condom], [A pouch lining the female tract; blocks sperm before they reach the cervix.],
  [Woman controls its use; also blocks STIs; slightly costlier.],
  [Diaphragm], [A flexible rubber dome fitted over the cervix — the mouth of the uterus.],
  [Reusable; must be fitted correctly and left in place after intercourse.],
  [Cervical cap], [A smaller cap covering only the cervix.],
  [Same idea as the diaphragm; needs a trained fitter for the right size.],
  [Spermicides], [Creams and jellies that kill sperm on contact.],
  [Used *with* diaphragms or caps; never reliable alone.],
)

#analogy-box(title: "Everyday Example — The Stadium Gate")[
  Barrier methods are the security gate of the reproductive tract: the match
  (fertilisation) can only happen if sperm get past the gate. A *condom* is a
  full gate with a collection bin attached, a *diaphragm* or *cervical cap*
  locks the entry door (the cervix), and a *spermicide* is the guard who
  removes any stragglers. Natural methods do something different — they
  simply keep the star player (the egg) off the field on match days.
]

