= Contraception II — IUDs, Pills, Sterilisation

Barrier methods work every single time they are used; devices, pills and
surgery work for months or years after a *single step*. Intrauterine devices
sit inside the womb for years; pills reset the body's hormone signals each
day; sterilisation closes the transport tubes once and for all. This is the
most "matching-heavy" part of the chapter — examiners pair each device with
its class and its action, so learn the families precisely.

== The three classes of IUDs

An *IUD (intrauterine device)* is a small device placed inside the uterus,
where it can stay for years. It is inserted by a doctor and works round the
clock — the couple does nothing.

#table(
  columns: (1.05fr, 1.05fr, 1.6fr),
  table.header[*Class*][*Named devices*][*How the class works*],
  [Non-medicated IUDs], [Lippes loop],
  [An inert plastic loop; its presence in the womb makes white blood cells engulf sperms (phagocytosis), so few sperms survive to fertilise the egg.],
  [Copper-releasing IUDs], [Cu-T, Cu-7, Multiload 375],
  [Copper ions released by the device *suppress sperm motility* (their ability to swim) and their fertilising capacity.],
  [Hormone-releasing IUDs], [Progestasert, LNG-20],
  [They release hormones that make the uterus unsuitable for *implantation* — the embedding of the early embryo; some also stop ovulation.],
)

- *IUD in one line:* a long-acting device inside the womb — but remember, an
  IUD does *not* protect against STIs.

== Pills: daily, weekly, emergency

#table(
  columns: (1.05fr, 1.35fr, 1.3fr),
  table.header[*Pill type*][*What it contains*][*How it is used*],
  [Oral contraceptive pills], [Norethindrone (a progestogen) plus ethinylestradiol (an oestrogen) — two hormones],
  [Taken daily; the hormone dose *inhibits ovulation*, so no egg is released to be fertilised.],
  [Saheli], [Centchroman — a *non-steroidal* pill],
  [Taken *once a week*; hardly any side effects; developed in India — a pride of Indian pharmacology.],
  [Emergency contraceptive pills], [High hormone doses],
  [Used within about *72 hours* after unprotected intercourse — an emergency rescue, *not* a routine method.],
)

- *Why hormonal pills work:* a steady supply of outside hormones fools the
  brain's cycle control, so the ovary holds its egg back.
- *Saheli's special place:* most pills are *steroidal* (hormone-based);
  Saheli is not, which is why its few side effects made it famous.

