= Sexually Transmitted Infections

Infections travel along more routes than one. Some ride on coughs and dirty
water; a stubborn group travels through *sexual contact*. These are the *STIs
(sexually transmitted infections)* — also called venereal diseases. They are
among the most common infectious diseases worldwide, and this chapter treats
them matter-of-factly: know the agent, know whether it is curable, know how
to prevent it. Health education, not embarrassment, is the answer.

- *The definition:* STIs are infections transmitted through sexual contact,
  from an infected partner to an uninfected one.
- *The early signs:* discharge from the genitals, itching, sores and pain in
  the pelvic region — but many infections show *no clear symptoms at first*,
  which is exactly why they spread silently.
- *The hidden damage:* untreated STIs can climb upward and cause *pelvic
  inflammatory disease* (long-term infection of the female tract), infertility,
  or complications in pregnancy such as stillbirth.
- *The silent-carrier rule:* a person can look and feel perfectly healthy and
  still carry and pass on an STI — so risk behaviour, not appearance, decides
  safety.

