= Infertility and ART

Some couples do everything right, yet month after month no pregnancy begins.
Doctors call it *infertility* when a couple fails to conceive after one to
two years of regular, unprotected attempts. It is more common than people
think, and — this is the sentence every student should carry home — *the cause
can sit with either partner*, the man or the woman, equally often. Modern
medicine has built a toolkit for exactly this problem, and this section
matches each tool to its job.

- *The causes:* they range from physical problems (blocked tubes) to
  congenital ones (present from birth), diseases, hormonal imbalances, certain
  drugs, and even stress.
- *The diagnosis rule:* *both* partners are examined — blaming the woman by
  default is a myth the WHO definition has buried.
- *The simpler steps first:* *hormonal treatment* can correct imbalances, and
  *artificial insemination* — collecting semen and placing it into the female
  tract by a doctor — can succeed without anything elaborate.

== When nature needs a helper: the ART toolkit

When simpler steps fail, couples turn to *ART (assisted reproductive
technologies)* — special techniques that handle the egg, the sperm or the
early embryo in the laboratory. Learn each method as three questions: *what
is collected or transferred, where does it go, and when does it fit?*

- *IVF — In Vitro Fertilisation:* "in vitro" means in glass. Eggs and sperm
  meet *outside* the body, and the embryo is transferred into the uterus once
  it has *8 or more blastomeres* (cells). Every famous "test-tube baby" began
  this way.
- *ZIFT — Zygote Intrafallopian Transfer:* the *zygote* (the freshly fertilised
  single cell) or an early embryo of *up to 8 blastomeres* is placed into the
  *fallopian tube*, and travels to the uterus on its own.
- *GIFT — Gamete Intrafallopian Transfer:* no lab fertilisation at all — the
  *gametes* (an egg and sperm) are placed into the fallopian tube, and
  fertilisation happens *inside the body*.
- *ICSI — Intra Cytoplasmic Sperm Injection:* one sperm is picked up with a
  fine needle and *injected directly into the cytoplasm of the egg* — the
  answer when sperm cannot enter the egg by themselves.
- *IUI — Intra Uterine Insemination:* prepared (washed, concentrated) semen
  is placed *into the uterus* — the simplest ART, used for low sperm count or
  poor sperm quality.

