#v(4pt)

#worked-example("Worked Example — Choosing the Right ART")[
  Match each couple to the technology a specialist would consider first.

  - *Couple A — the woman's fallopian tubes are irreversibly blocked:*
    sperm and egg cannot meet in the tube at all, so fertilisation must
    happen outside — *IVF*, with the 8-or-more-blastomere embryo placed in
    the uterus.
  - *Couple B — the man has a low sperm count:* the simplest boost first —
    *IUI*, placing prepared semen straight into the uterus.
  - *Couple C — the sperm cannot penetrate the egg on their own:* no amount
    of proximity will help; a single sperm must be injected into the egg —
    *ICSI*.
  - *Couple D — healthy tubes, and doctors want the embryo to develop in the
    tube's natural setting:* transfer the early stage into the tube —
    *ZIFT* (up to 8 blastomeres) or *GIFT* (gametes, fertilisation inside).
]

== The complete answers beyond technology

- *Counselling:* both partners carry stress and guilt; professional
  counselling supports couples through every stage of treatment.
- *Surrogacy:* a *surrogate mother* carries the couple's baby in her own
  uterus when the woman cannot — an arrangement India now regulates by law.
- *Adoption:* a completely valid way to build a family, and the chapter's
  final message — a family is built by love, not only by biology.
- *The regulation one-liner:* India's *ART Act, 2021* registers clinics, sets
  ethical standards and protects the rights of everyone involved.

